          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-10000-on-a-funeral/">
  <strong>$10,000 Funeral Calculator</strong>

  <span>
    See how unexpected major expenses interact with debt pressure,
    savings, and monthly flexibility.
  </span>
</a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/budget-rule-calculators/">
          <strong>Budget Rule Calculators</strong>
          <span>
            Browse percentage-based calculators and financial decision guides.
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>


    <section id="DebtBoundaryExamples">
      <h2>Debt is counted because it competes with the next decision.</h2>

      <p>
        The point of counting debt is not to label a household as good or bad with
        money. The point is to see how much of the next paycheck is already claimed
        before a new car payment, rent increase, trip, medical bill, wedding cost, or
        home repair gets added.
      </p>

      <p>
        A $300 monthly debt payment can matter more than a $5,000 balance if the
        calculator is judging monthly breathing room. A $5,000 balance can matter
        more than the minimum payment if the calculator is judging total financial
        exposure, payoff time, or whether emergency savings would survive a new cost.
      </p>

      <div class="debt-decision-grid">
        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Count required payments</h3>
          <p>
            If the payment must be made on schedule, it reduces flexibility. That
            usually includes credit cards, car loans, student loans, personal loans,
            medical payment plans, buy-now-pay-later plans, and past-due balances.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Separate normal bills</h3>
          <p>
            Rent, groceries, utilities, insurance premiums, subscriptions, and phone
            bills are usually expenses rather than debt. They still matter, but they
            should be entered where a calculator asks for monthly bills or expenses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Watch payment plans</h3>
          <p>
            A bill can become debt when it turns into scheduled repayment. A medical
            bill, tax balance, furniture financing plan, or installment agreement may
            behave like debt even if it did not start as a traditional loan.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="MonthlyPaymentVsBalance">
      <h2>Use monthly payments when the calculator is testing cash flow.</h2>

      <p>
        Many ShouldISpend calculators ask for monthly debt payments because the result
        depends on what is left each month. In that case, enter the required payment,
        not the full balance. A $12,000 car loan with a $420 payment usually affects
        monthly affordability through the $420 payment, plus insurance, fuel, repairs,
        and other vehicle costs.
      </p>

      <p>
        Full balances matter when the question is about payoff risk, total debt load,
        interest exposure, or whether savings should be used to reduce debt. The label
        on the calculator is the guide: monthly debt payments and total debt balance
        are related, but they answer different questions.
      </p>

      <ul class="debt-check-list">
        <li>For monthly affordability, enter the required monthly payments.</li>
        <li>For payoff or total debt pressure, enter the full balances requested.</li>
        <li>For credit cards, count carried balances and the payment needed to avoid falling behind.</li>
        <li>For buy-now-pay-later, count the scheduled payments that overlap the new decision.</li>
        <li>For co-signed debt, count it if the payment could realistically become your responsibility.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="DebtEdgeCases">
      <h2>Debt edge cases can change the answer.</h2>

      <p>
        Some obligations sit between a normal bill and formal debt. These gray areas
        are worth slowing down for because they can make a spending decision look safer
        than it really is.
      </p>

      <div class="debt-decision-grid">
        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Collections and past-due bills</h3>
          <p>
            A past-due utility bill, medical bill, or old account in collections may
            not feel like a normal loan, but it can still create repayment pressure,
            fees, calls, credit damage, or legal risk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Deferred student loans</h3>
          <p>
            A deferred loan may not hurt this month, but it can matter if payments
            resume soon. A safer estimate includes the future payment when the new
            spending commitment will overlap repayment.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Zero-interest financing</h3>
          <p>
            Zero-interest debt is still a claim on future income. The risk is higher
            if the promotional period ends, the payment crowds out savings, or the
            balance would become expensive if not paid on time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="debt-card">
          <h3>Shared or household debt</h3>
          <p>
            If a partner, spouse, roommate, or family member is responsible for part
            of the payment, count the part your household actually has to support and
            be conservative when that support is uncertain.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="AvoidDebtDoubleCounting">
      <h2>Avoid double-counting the same obligation.</h2>

      <p>
        Debt should be counted once in the right place. If a calculator asks for rent,
        normal monthly expenses, and debt payments separately, do not include the same
        housing payment in all three boxes. If a calculator asks for total monthly
        expenses including debt, then include the debt there and avoid entering it a
        second time unless the page specifically asks for a separate debt field.
      </p>

      <div class="debt-callout">
        <p>
          Conservative is safer than perfect. When you are unsure, count any payment
          that would still be due after the new purchase, and read the calculator label
          carefully before deciding whether to enter the monthly payment, the full
          balance, or the broader monthly expense.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="DebtRelatedCalculators">
      <h2>Debt connects to most affordability decisions.</h2>

      <p>
        Debt pressure matters because it changes how much room is left for housing,
        transportation, emergency savings, family costs, healthcare, and major planned
        purchases. These related tools use debt in different ways.
      </p>

      <div class="debt-link-grid">
        <a class="debt-link-card" href="/how-much-debt-is-too-much/">
          <strong>How Much Debt Is Too Much?</strong>
          <span>Compare debt payments with income, fixed bills, savings, and monthly flexibility.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="debt-link-card" href="/budget-rule-calculators/">
          <strong>Budget Rule Calculators</strong>
          <span>Use debt, rent, savings, and take-home pay rules as starting points, not permission slips.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="debt-link-card" href="/emergency-savings-calculators/">
          <strong>Emergency Savings Calculators</strong>
          <span>See how debt payoff and cash cushion decisions compete for the same dollars.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="debt-link-card" href="/rent-calculators/">
          <strong>Rent Calculators</strong>
          <span>Test housing costs alongside debt, utilities, deposits, and monthly breathing room.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="debt-link-card" href="/car-calculators/">
          <strong>Car Calculators</strong>
          <span>Compare car payments with debt, insurance, fuel, maintenance, and cash-flow risk.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="debt-link-card" href="/medical-calculators/">
          <strong>Medical Cost Calculators</strong>
          <span>Review medical bills, payment plans, insurance exposure, and debt pressure together.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <FAQSection title="Debt Basics FAQ" faqs={debtFaqs} />
  </main>
</Layout>